{"name":"Olde World Gold","hex":"#8f6c3e","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"olde","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Archaic spelling of old."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/olde"},{"term":"world","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The subjective human experience, regarded collectively; human collective existence; existence in general; the reality we live in.","The subjective human experience, regarded individually."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To consider or cause to be considered from a global perspective; to consider as a global whole, rather than making or focusing on national or other distinctions; compare globalize.","To make real; to make worldly."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/world"},{"term":"gold","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A heavy yellow elemental metal of great value, with atomic number 79.","A coin or coinage made of this material, or supposedly so."]},{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["☉ (alchemy)"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/gold"}],"entries":[{"title":"Olde","blocks":[{"text":"Olde is a surname. Notable people with the surname include:"},{"items":["Barney Olde (1882–1932), Australian politician","Erika Olde, Canadian film producer, financier and billionaire heiress","Hans Olde (1855–1917), German painter and art school administrator","Margareth Olde, Estonian chess Woman FIDE Master","Neil Leverne Olde (1904–1969), Canadian politician"]}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Olde","wikidata":"Q45083482"},{"title":"World","blocks":[{"text":"A world is a totality of entities. In philosophy, the world is customarily viewed as all of existence or the whole of reality. Some conceptions see the world as unique, while others examine the character of entities that exist in the world or talk of a plurality of worlds, challenging the idea that the world is an all-inclusive whole. The term is also commonly used as a context-dependent expression to denote a specific domain of discourse and everything within it, such as the Earth and all life on it or the whole of human existence."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2f/Hubble_ultra_deep_field.jpg/330px-Hubble_ultra_deep_field.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/World","wikidata":"Q16502"},{"title":"Gold","blocks":[{"text":"Gold is a chemical element; its chemical symbol is Au and atomic number 79. In its pure form, it is a bright-metallic-yellow, dense, soft, malleable, and ductile metal. Chemically, gold is a transition metal, a group 11 element, and one of the noble metals. It is one of the least reactive chemical elements, being the second lowest in the reactivity series, with only platinum ranked as less reactive. Gold is solid under standard conditions."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/Gold-crystals.jpg/330px-Gold-crystals.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold","wikidata":"Q897"},{"title":"Gold (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Gold, also called golden, is a color tone resembling the chemical element gold."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/GoldNuggetUSGOV.jpg/330px-GoldNuggetUSGOV.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold_(color)","wikidata":"Q208045"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/Yggdrasil.jpg/500px-Yggdrasil.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/Yggdrasil.jpg/500px-Yggdrasil.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Yggdrasil, an attempt to reconstruct the Norse world tree which connects the heavens, the world, and the underworld.",